                    coach_r3_trim:          `<strong>Trimming to benchmark cuts the risk and the carbon</strong> — clean and certain. The price is a realized gain and trading cost on a rallied book, and you give up the upside if the rally continues. Defensible, but check whether an overlay would have cut the same downside without realizing the gain.`,
                    coach_r3_hedge:         `<strong>An overlay cuts the downside without realizing the gain</strong> — the risk-efficient move. You kept the equities and the factor exposure while pulling VaR back under the cap. Just keep the instrument count lean: precision you do not need is cost you cannot afford on a fund already behind.`,
                    coach_r3_hedgethin:     `<strong>A light overlay barely moves the risk.</strong> At this ratio you have paid for an instrument without buying enough VaR reduction to clear the cap. Either raise the hedge ratio or accept that the energy book is still driving your downside.`,
                    coach_r3_overhedge:     `<strong>Beautiful overlay — three instruments, fully neutralized energy beta.</strong> It also cost you ~45 bps in market impact on a fund that is already behind. Did the precision buy enough risk reduction to justify the bleed, or did you just hedge your Sharpe ratio into the floor? More hedge is not always more.`,
                    coach_r3_rotate:        `<strong>Rotating into transition names is the carbon win</strong> — you cut the carbon breach hard while keeping energy-factor exposure, so you do not blame the screen for a bet you can simply re-shape. Watch the realized cost of the swap, but this is the move that satisfies ESG and risk together.`,

                    coach_r4_steward:       `<strong>Compliance plus a stewardship story is what retains the mandate.</strong> The book clears all four limits and you can explain why the breach happened and what changes so it does not recur. That is the difference between a manager the committee tolerates and one it trusts with US$900M.`,
                    coach_r4_stewardnoncompliant: `<strong>Good stewardship narrative — but the book is not yet compliant.</strong> A story cannot cure a live limit breach. One of ESG, tracking error, VaR or carbon is still outside its limit, and the committee will see it before they hear the stewardship plan. Fix the breach first.`,
                    coach_r4_numbers:       `<strong>Your portfolio is compliant — congratulations.</strong> But the chair just asked why she should keep 900 million dollars with a manager who let the ESG score breach in the first place. Numbers without a stewardship story tell her you fixed the symptom, not the process. What changes so this does not recur next quarter?`,
                    coach_r4_defiant:       `<strong>Arguing the client should loosen the ESG floor is the wrong room to pick that fight.</strong> The floor is in the investment management agreement; telling the committee the screen is the problem confirms exactly the conflict you were meant to disprove. Show the decomposition instead.`,
                    coach_r4_separable:     `<strong>That proves Zavaleta's point with this quarter's numbers.</strong> You showed the −65 bps was a separable energy-factor exposure you hedged back, cleared all four limits, and led with stewardship. Responsibility and return were not in conflict — you evidenced it rather than asserting it. The mandate stays.`,
                    coach_r4_tradeoff:      `<strong>Conceding that responsibility costs return hands the client the argument to drop the screen.</strong> If the underperformance was really a hedgeable factor bet, say so and show the decomposition — do not accept a trade-off you did not actually have to make.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>“Responsibility is its own reward” is not an answer to a fiduciary committee.</strong> They have a duty to the pension's beneficiaries; they need to see that the screen did not cost them return this quarter, with numbers. Belief without evidence reads as greenwashing.`,
                    coach_r4_misprioritize: `<strong>Return and tracking error are not the two that lose you the mandate.</strong> The contractual ESG breach and the hard VaR ceiling are the commitments the client's board will act on. Prioritizing performance over the two binding limits is how you clear the wrong constraints.`,
                    coach_r4_refusechoose:  `<strong>Refusing to prioritize is a non-answer.</strong> The chair asked which two are non-negotiable for the client precisely to see whether you understand their risk appetite. The ESG floor and the VaR limit are the ones that trigger a mandate review; name them.`,

